WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.480
We covered four types of topologies in the last four topics: star, mesh, ring, and bus.

00:08.480 --> 00:16.273
There is one more aspect to understand about topologies: the difference between a physical topology and a logical topology.

00:16.273 --> 00:20.223
A physical topology is the actual appearance and setup of a network.

00:20.223 --> 00:29.892
For example, if you have a device such as a switch or a hub in the middle of a network and devices connected to it, you have a physical star network.

00:29.892 --> 00:39.101
However, that does not mean the data flows through the network as if it were a star network. This is where the concept of a logical topology comes in.

00:39.101 --> 00:43.461
A logical topology describes how data flows through a network.

00:43.461 --> 00:48.418
For example, in the days of Token Ring networks, the physical setup was a star,

00:48.418 --> 00:54.797
but data flowed through the network as if it were a ring, with a single token bouncing from device to device.

00:54.797 --> 01:01.016
And Ethernet networks, even though usually a physical star, actually flow logically as a bus topology.

01:01.016 --> 01:08.759
For the exam, know that there is a difference between a physical and a logical topology.

